,"gene_symbol_current":"MRPS5","gene_id":"ENSG00000144029","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.618832,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0187303,"nucleus_score":0.6694,"pct_ad":0.130166,"pct_control":0.125995,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.955709,"donor_log2fc":0.0406392,"donor_lfcse":0.107604,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00318752}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000144029","module":"magenta","kme":0.150708,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.656828,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000144029","module":"turquoise","kme":0.227485,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.446504,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000144029","module":"turquoise","kme":0.148016,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.704234,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000144029","entrez_id":["64969"],"uniprot":["P82675"],"ncbi_summary":"Mammalian mitochondrial ribosomal proteins are encoded by nuclear genes and help in protein synthesis within the mitochondrion. Mitochondrial ribosomes (mitoribosomes) consist of a small 28S subunit and a large 39S subunit. They have an estimated 75% protein to rRNA composition compared to prokaryotic ribosomes, where this ratio is reversed. Another difference between mammalian mitoribosomes and prokaryotic ribosomes is that the latter contain a 5S rRNA. Among different species, the proteins comprising the mitoribosome differ greatly in sequence, and sometimes in biochemical properties, which prevents easy recognition by sequence homology. This gene encodes a 28S subunit protein that belongs to the ribosomal protein S5P family. Pseudogenes corresponding to this gene are found on chromosomes 4q, 5q, and 18q. [provided by RefSeq, Jul 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info"